1
00:00:04,399 --> 00:00:08,399
Mr. Pumblechook’s premises in the High Street of the market town,

2
00:00:08,399 --> 00:00:14,079
were of a peppercorny and farinaceous character, as the premises of a cornchandler and seedsman

3
00:00:14,079 --> 00:00:18,720
should be. It appeared to me that he must be a very happy man indeed,

4
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:23,039
to have so many little drawers in his shop; and I wondered when I peeped into

5
00:00:23,039 --> 00:00:28,960
one or two on the lower tiers, and saw the tied-up brown paper packets inside,

6
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:36,840
whether the flower-seeds and bulbs ever wanted of a fine day to break out of those jails, and bloom.

7
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:42,319
It was in the early morning after my arrival that I entertained this speculation. On the

8
00:00:42,319 --> 00:00:47,720
previous night, I had been sent straight to bed in an attic with a sloping roof,

9
00:00:47,720 --> 00:00:52,840
which was so low in the corner where the bedstead was, that I calculated the tiles as being within

10
00:00:52,840 --> 00:00:59,079
a foot of my eyebrows. In the same early morning, I discovered a singular affinity

11
00:00:59,079 --> 00:01:07,159
between seeds and corduroys. Mr. Pumblechook wore corduroys, and so did his shopman; and somehow,

12
00:01:07,159 --> 00:01:12,720
there was a general air and flavour about the corduroys, so much in the nature of seeds,

13
00:01:12,720 --> 00:01:17,840
and a general air and flavour about the seeds, so much in the nature of corduroys,

14
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:23,600
that I hardly knew which was which. The same opportunity served me for noticing that Mr.

15
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:28,960
Pumblechook appeared to conduct his business by looking across the street at the saddler,

16
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:33,000
who appeared to transact his business by keeping his eye on the coachmaker,

17
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:38,119
who appeared to get on in life by putting his hands in his pockets and contemplating the baker,

18
00:01:38,119 --> 00:01:42,920
who in his turn folded his arms and stared at the grocer, who stood at his door and yawned at

19
00:01:42,920 --> 00:01:49,680
the chemist. The watchmaker, always poring over a little desk with a magnifying-glass at his eye,

20
00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:54,079
and always inspected by a group of smock-frocks poring over him through the glass of his

21
00:01:54,079 --> 00:02:01,479
shop-window, seemed to be about the only person in the High Street whose trade engaged his attention.

22
00:02:01,479 --> 00:02:06,640
Mr. Pumblechook and I breakfasted at eight o’clock in the parlour behind the shop,

23
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:11,520
while the shopman took his mug of tea and hunch of bread and butter on a sack of peas in the front

24
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:18,920
premises. I considered Mr. Pumblechook wretched company. Besides being possessed by my sister’s

25
00:02:18,920 --> 00:02:25,720
idea that a mortifying and penitential character ought to be imparted to my diet,—besides giving me

26
00:02:25,720 --> 00:02:31,479
as much crumb as possible in combination with as little butter, and putting such a quantity of warm

27
00:02:31,479 --> 00:02:36,640
water into my milk that it would have been more candid to have left the milk out altogether,—his

28
00:02:36,640 --> 00:02:42,760
conversation consisted of nothing but arithmetic. On my politely bidding him Good-morning, he said,

29
00:02:42,760 --> 00:02:50,520
pompously, “Seven times nine, boy?” And how should I be able to answer, dodged in that way, in a

30
00:02:50,520 --> 00:02:57,319
strange place, on an empty stomach! I was hungry, but before I had swallowed a morsel, he began a

31
00:02:57,319 --> 00:03:05,239
running sum that lasted all through the breakfast. “Seven?” “And four?” “And eight?” “And six?” “And

32
00:03:05,239 --> 00:03:12,279
two?” “And ten?” And so on. And after each figure was disposed of, it was as much as I could do to

33
00:03:12,279 --> 00:03:19,159
get a bite or a sup, before the next came; while he sat at his ease guessing nothing, and eating

34
00:03:19,159 --> 00:03:26,399
bacon and hot roll, in (if I may be allowed the expression) a gorging and gormandizing manner.

35
00:03:26,399 --> 00:03:32,079
For such reasons, I was very glad when ten o’clock came and we started for Miss Havisham’s;

36
00:03:32,079 --> 00:03:36,800
though I was not at all at my ease regarding the manner in which I should acquit myself under that

37
00:03:36,800 --> 00:03:44,399
lady’s roof. Within a quarter of an hour we came to Miss Havisham’s house, which was of old brick,

38
00:03:44,399 --> 00:03:52,039
and dismal, and had a great many iron bars to it. Some of the windows had been walled up;

39
00:03:52,039 --> 00:03:58,680
of those that remained, all the lower were rustily barred. There was a courtyard in front,

40
00:03:58,680 --> 00:04:03,760
and that was barred; so we had to wait, after ringing the bell, until some one should come

41
00:04:03,760 --> 00:04:10,399
to open it. While we waited at the gate, I peeped in (even then Mr. Pumblechook said,

42
00:04:10,399 --> 00:04:16,640
“And fourteen?” but I pretended not to hear him), and saw that at the side of the house there was a

43
00:04:16,640 --> 00:04:24,279
large brewery. No brewing was going on in it, and none seemed to have gone on for a long long time.

44
00:04:24,279 --> 00:04:30,800
A window was raised, and a clear voice demanded “What name?” To which my conductor replied,

45
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:36,960
“Pumblechook.” The voice returned, “Quite right,” and the window was shut again,

46
00:04:36,960 --> 00:04:41,520
and a young lady came across the court-yard, with keys in her hand.

47
00:04:41,520 --> 00:04:45,720
“This,” said Mr. Pumblechook, “is Pip.”

48
00:04:45,720 --> 00:04:48,520
“This is Pip, is it?” returned the young lady,

49
00:04:48,520 --> 00:04:53,159
who was very pretty and seemed very proud; “come in, Pip.”

50
00:04:53,159 --> 00:04:57,960
Mr. Pumblechook was coming in also, when she stopped him with the gate.

51
00:04:57,960 --> 00:05:01,800
“Oh!” she said. “Did you wish to see Miss Havisham?”

52
00:05:01,800 --> 00:05:06,880
“If Miss Havisham wished to see me,” returned Mr. Pumblechook, discomfited.

53
00:05:06,880 --> 00:05:13,000
“Ah!” said the girl; “but you see she don’t.” She said it so finally, and in such an

54
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:18,960
undiscussible way, that Mr. Pumblechook, though in a condition of ruffled dignity,

55
00:05:18,960 --> 00:05:25,720
could not protest. But he eyed me severely,—as if I had done anything to him!—and departed with

56
00:05:25,720 --> 00:05:32,960
the words reproachfully delivered: “Boy! Let your behaviour here be a credit unto them which brought

57
00:05:32,960 --> 00:05:38,479
you up by hand!” I was not free from apprehension that he would come back to propound through the

58
00:05:38,479 --> 00:05:45,720
gate, “And sixteen?” But he didn’t. My young conductress locked the gate,

59
00:05:45,720 --> 00:05:50,399
and we went across the courtyard. It was paved and clean, but grass was growing in

60
00:05:50,399 --> 00:05:55,359
every crevice. The brewery buildings had a little lane of communication with it,

61
00:05:55,359 --> 00:06:00,479
and the wooden gates of that lane stood open, and all the brewery beyond stood open,

62
00:06:00,479 --> 00:06:06,880
away to the high enclosing wall; and all was empty and disused. The cold wind seemed

63
00:06:06,880 --> 00:06:12,000
to blow colder there than outside the gate; and it made a shrill noise in howling in and

64
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:17,800
out at the open sides of the brewery, like the noise of wind in the rigging of a ship at sea.

65
00:06:17,800 --> 00:06:20,560
She saw me looking at it, and she said,

66
00:06:20,560 --> 00:06:25,159
“You could drink without hurt all the strong beer that’s brewed there now, boy.”

67
00:06:25,159 --> 00:06:29,279
“I should think I could, miss,” said I, in a shy way.

68
00:06:29,279 --> 00:06:34,720
“Better not try to brew beer there now, or it would turn out sour, boy; don’t you think so?”

69
00:06:34,720 --> 00:06:37,039
“It looks like it, miss.”

70
00:06:37,039 --> 00:06:42,000
“Not that anybody means to try,” she added, “for that’s all done with,

71
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:47,279
and the place will stand as idle as it is till it falls. As to strong beer,

72
00:06:47,279 --> 00:06:51,600
there’s enough of it in the cellars already, to drown the Manor House.”

73
00:06:51,600 --> 00:06:54,199
“Is that the name of this house, miss?”

74
00:06:54,199 --> 00:06:56,760
“One of its names, boy.”

75
00:06:56,760 --> 00:06:59,319
“It has more than one, then, miss?”

76
00:06:59,319 --> 00:07:04,840
“One more. Its other name was Satis; which is Greek, or Latin, or Hebrew,

77
00:07:04,840 --> 00:07:08,520
or all three—or all one to me—for enough.”

78
00:07:08,520 --> 00:07:12,920
“Enough House,” said I; “that’s a curious name, miss.”

79
00:07:12,920 --> 00:07:18,640
“Yes,” she replied; “but it meant more than it said. It meant, when it was given,

80
00:07:18,640 --> 00:07:22,279
that whoever had this house could want nothing else. They must have

81
00:07:22,279 --> 00:07:28,680
been easily satisfied in those days, I should think. But don’t loiter, boy.”

82
00:07:28,680 --> 00:07:33,960
Though she called me “boy” so often, and with a carelessness that was far from complimentary,

83
00:07:33,960 --> 00:07:40,680
she was of about my own age. She seemed much older than I, of course, being a girl, and beautiful and

84
00:07:40,680 --> 00:07:48,119
self-possessed; and she was as scornful of me as if she had been one-and-twenty, and a queen.

85
00:07:48,119 --> 00:07:52,840
We went into the house by a side door, the great front entrance had two chains across

86
00:07:52,840 --> 00:07:58,800
it outside,—and the first thing I noticed was, that the passages were all dark,

87
00:07:58,800 --> 00:08:03,800
and that she had left a candle burning there. She took it up, and we went through

88
00:08:03,800 --> 00:08:11,039
more passages and up a staircase, and still it was all dark, and only the candle lighted us.

89
00:08:11,039 --> 00:08:16,640
At last we came to the door of a room, and she said, “Go in.”

90
00:08:16,640 --> 00:08:22,039
I answered, more in shyness than politeness, “After you, miss.”

91
00:08:22,039 --> 00:08:25,600
To this she returned: “Don’t be ridiculous, boy;

92
00:08:25,600 --> 00:08:33,640
I am not going in.” And scornfully walked away, and—what was worse—took the candle with her.

93
00:08:33,640 --> 00:08:38,920
This was very uncomfortable, and I was half afraid. However, the only thing to be done

94
00:08:38,920 --> 00:08:45,399
being to knock at the door, I knocked, and was told from within to enter. I entered, therefore,

95
00:08:45,399 --> 00:08:51,520
and found myself in a pretty large room, well lighted with wax candles. No glimpse of daylight

96
00:08:51,520 --> 00:08:56,560
was to be seen in it. It was a dressing-room, as I supposed from the furniture, though much

97
00:08:56,560 --> 00:09:02,840
of it was of forms and uses then quite unknown to me. But prominent in it was a draped table

98
00:09:02,840 --> 00:09:09,399
with a gilded looking-glass, and that I made out at first sight to be a fine lady’s dressing-table.

99
00:09:09,399 --> 00:09:13,600
Whether I should have made out this object so soon if there had been no fine lady sitting at it,

100
00:09:13,600 --> 00:09:19,239
I cannot say. In an arm-chair, with an elbow resting on the table and her head

101
00:09:19,239 --> 00:09:24,640
leaning on that hand, sat the strangest lady I have ever seen, or shall ever see.

102
00:09:25,960 --> 00:09:32,479
She was dressed in rich materials,—satins, and lace, and silks,—all of white. Her shoes were

103
00:09:32,479 --> 00:09:37,960
white. And she had a long white veil dependent from her hair, and she had bridal flowers in

104
00:09:37,960 --> 00:09:44,399
her hair, but her hair was white. Some bright jewels sparkled on her neck and on her hands,

105
00:09:44,399 --> 00:09:51,039
and some other jewels lay sparkling on the table. Dresses, less splendid than the dress she wore,

106
00:09:51,039 --> 00:09:56,880
and half-packed trunks, were scattered about. She had not quite finished dressing, for she

107
00:09:56,880 --> 00:10:03,039
had but one shoe on,—the other was on the table near her hand,—her veil was but half arranged,

108
00:10:03,039 --> 00:10:08,119
her watch and chain were not put on, and some lace for her bosom lay with those trinkets,

109
00:10:08,119 --> 00:10:12,560
and with her handkerchief, and gloves, and some flowers, and a Prayer-Book all

110
00:10:12,560 --> 00:10:18,119
confusedly heaped about the looking-glass. It was not in the first few moments that I

111
00:10:18,119 --> 00:10:24,680
saw all these things, though I saw more of them in the first moments than might be supposed. But

112
00:10:24,680 --> 00:10:30,359
I saw that everything within my view which ought to be white, had been white long ago,

113
00:10:30,359 --> 00:10:37,079
and had lost its lustre and was faded and yellow. I saw that the bride within the bridal dress had

114
00:10:37,079 --> 00:10:42,520
withered like the dress, and like the flowers, and had no brightness left but the brightness of

115
00:10:42,520 --> 00:10:48,800
her sunken eyes. I saw that the dress had been put upon the rounded figure of a young woman,

116
00:10:48,800 --> 00:10:55,239
and that the figure upon which it now hung loose had shrunk to skin and bone. Once,

117
00:10:55,239 --> 00:11:01,159
I had been taken to see some ghastly waxwork at the Fair, representing I know not what impossible

118
00:11:01,159 --> 00:11:07,680
personage lying in state. Once, I had been taken to one of our old marsh churches to see a skeleton

119
00:11:07,680 --> 00:11:13,800
in the ashes of a rich dress that had been dug out of a vault under the church pavement. Now,

120
00:11:13,800 --> 00:11:17,680
waxwork and skeleton seemed to have dark eyes that moved and looked at

121
00:11:17,680 --> 00:11:23,359
me. I should have cried out, if I could. “Who is it?” said the lady at the table.

122
00:11:24,680 --> 00:11:25,869
“Pip, ma’am.”

123
00:11:25,869 --> 00:11:29,760
“Pip?” “Mr. Pumblechook’s boy, ma’am. Come—to play.”

124
00:11:29,760 --> 00:11:33,880
“Come nearer; let me look at you. Come close.”

125
00:11:33,880 --> 00:11:38,439
It was when I stood before her, avoiding her eyes, that I took note of the surrounding

126
00:11:38,439 --> 00:11:44,600
objects in detail, and saw that her watch had stopped at twenty minutes to nine,

127
00:11:44,600 --> 00:11:49,720
and that a clock in the room had stopped at twenty minutes to nine.

128
00:11:49,720 --> 00:11:53,199
“Look at me,” said Miss Havisham. “You are not afraid of a woman who

129
00:11:53,199 --> 00:11:56,399
has never seen the sun since you were born?”

130
00:11:56,399 --> 00:12:03,560
I regret to state that I was not afraid of telling the enormous lie comprehended in the answer “No.”

131
00:12:03,560 --> 00:12:09,640
“Do you know what I touch here?” she said, laying her hands, one upon the other, on her left side.

132
00:12:09,640 --> 00:12:13,560
“Yes, ma’am.” (It made me think of the young man.)

133
00:12:13,560 --> 00:12:15,359
“What do I touch?”

134
00:12:15,359 --> 00:12:16,720
“Your heart.”

135
00:12:16,720 --> 00:12:18,520
“Broken!”

136
00:12:18,520 --> 00:12:22,640
She uttered the word with an eager look, and with strong emphasis,

137
00:12:22,640 --> 00:12:28,640
and with a weird smile that had a kind of boast in it. Afterwards she kept her hands

138
00:12:28,640 --> 00:12:33,680
there for a little while, and slowly took them away as if they were heavy.

139
00:12:33,680 --> 00:12:37,119
“I am tired,” said Miss Havisham. “I want diversion,

140
00:12:37,119 --> 00:12:41,199
and I have done with men and women. Play.”

141
00:12:41,199 --> 00:12:45,239
I think it will be conceded by my most disputatious reader,

142
00:12:45,239 --> 00:12:49,079
that she could hardly have directed an unfortunate boy to do anything in

143
00:12:49,079 --> 00:12:54,600
the wide world more difficult to be done under the circumstances.

144
00:12:54,600 --> 00:13:02,159
“I sometimes have sick fancies,” she went on, “and I have a sick fancy that I want to see some play.

145
00:13:02,159 --> 00:13:09,960
There, there!” with an impatient movement of the fingers of her right hand; “play, play, play!”

146
00:13:09,960 --> 00:13:14,039
For a moment, with the fear of my sister’s working me before my eyes,

147
00:13:14,039 --> 00:13:18,520
I had a desperate idea of starting round the room in the assumed character of Mr.

148
00:13:18,520 --> 00:13:24,600
Pumblechook’s chaise-cart. But I felt myself so unequal to the performance that I gave it up,

149
00:13:24,600 --> 00:13:29,359
and stood looking at Miss Havisham in what I suppose she took for a dogged manner,

150
00:13:29,359 --> 00:13:33,399
inasmuch as she said, when we had taken a good look at each other,—

151
00:13:33,399 --> 00:13:36,039
“Are you sullen and obstinate?”

152
00:13:36,039 --> 00:13:41,760
“No, ma’am, I am very sorry for you, and very sorry I can’t play just now. If you complain

153
00:13:41,760 --> 00:13:46,560
of me I shall get into trouble with my sister, so I would do it if I could;

154
00:13:46,560 --> 00:13:54,399
but it’s so new here, and so strange, and so fine,—and melancholy—.” I stopped,

155
00:13:54,399 --> 00:14:00,359
fearing I might say too much, or had already said it, and we took another look at each other.

156
00:14:00,359 --> 00:14:05,239
Before she spoke again, she turned her eyes from me, and looked at the dress she wore,

157
00:14:05,239 --> 00:14:10,720
and at the dressing-table, and finally at herself in the looking-glass.

158
00:14:10,720 --> 00:14:13,600
“So new to him,” she muttered, “so old to me;

159
00:14:13,600 --> 00:14:19,880
so strange to him, so familiar to me; so melancholy to both of us! Call Estella.”

160
00:14:19,880 --> 00:14:22,520
As she was still looking at the reflection of herself,

161
00:14:22,520 --> 00:14:26,960
I thought she was still talking to herself, and kept quiet.

162
00:14:26,960 --> 00:14:34,840
“Call Estella,” she repeated, flashing a look at me. “You can do that. Call Estella. At the door.”

163
00:14:34,840 --> 00:14:38,680
To stand in the dark in a mysterious passage of an unknown house,

164
00:14:38,680 --> 00:14:43,600
bawling Estella to a scornful young lady neither visible nor responsive,

165
00:14:43,600 --> 00:14:50,159
and feeling it a dreadful liberty so to roar out her name, was almost as bad as playing to order.

166
00:14:50,159 --> 00:14:56,039
But she answered at last, and her light came along the dark passage like a star.

167
00:14:56,039 --> 00:15:00,800
Miss Havisham beckoned her to come close, and took up a jewel from the table,

168
00:15:00,800 --> 00:15:07,159
and tried its effect upon her fair young bosom and against her pretty brown hair. “Your own,

169
00:15:07,159 --> 00:15:13,359
one day, my dear, and you will use it well. Let me see you play cards with this boy.”

170
00:15:13,359 --> 00:15:17,720
“With this boy? Why, he is a common labouring-boy!”

171
00:15:17,720 --> 00:15:20,720
I thought I overheard Miss Havisham answer,—only

172
00:15:20,720 --> 00:15:26,000
it seemed so unlikely,—“Well? You can break his heart.”

173
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:31,079
“What do you play, boy?” asked Estella of myself, with the greatest disdain.

174
00:15:31,079 --> 00:15:33,560
“Nothing but beggar my neighbour, miss.”

175
00:15:33,560 --> 00:15:40,520
“Beggar him,” said Miss Havisham to Estella. So we sat down to cards.

176
00:15:40,520 --> 00:15:43,920
It was then I began to understand that everything in the room had stopped,

177
00:15:43,920 --> 00:15:49,159
like the watch and the clock, a long time ago. I noticed that Miss Havisham put down

178
00:15:49,159 --> 00:15:55,039
the jewel exactly on the spot from which she had taken it up. As Estella dealt the cards,

179
00:15:55,039 --> 00:16:01,279
I glanced at the dressing-table again, and saw that the shoe upon it, once white, now yellow,

180
00:16:01,279 --> 00:16:06,960
had never been worn. I glanced down at the foot from which the shoe was absent,

181
00:16:06,960 --> 00:16:11,279
and saw that the silk stocking on it, once white, now yellow, had been trodden

182
00:16:11,279 --> 00:16:18,439
ragged. Without this arrest of everything, this standing still of all the pale decayed objects,

183
00:16:18,439 --> 00:16:22,199
not even the withered bridal dress on the collapsed form could have looked so like

184
00:16:22,199 --> 00:16:30,560
grave-clothes, or the long veil so like a shroud. So she sat, corpse-like, as we played at cards;

185
00:16:30,560 --> 00:16:37,279
the frillings and trimmings on her bridal dress, looking like earthy paper. I knew nothing then of

186
00:16:37,279 --> 00:16:42,439
the discoveries that are occasionally made of bodies buried in ancient times, which fall to

187
00:16:42,439 --> 00:16:47,920
powder in the moment of being distinctly seen; but, I have often thought since, that she must

188
00:16:47,920 --> 00:16:53,199
have looked as if the admission of the natural light of day would have struck her to dust.

189
00:16:53,199 --> 00:16:58,079
“He calls the knaves Jacks, this boy!” said Estella with disdain,

190
00:16:58,079 --> 00:17:05,279
before our first game was out. “And what coarse hands he has! And what thick boots!”

191
00:17:05,279 --> 00:17:08,319
I had never thought of being ashamed of my hands before;

192
00:17:08,319 --> 00:17:12,720
but I began to consider them a very indifferent pair. Her contempt for me

193
00:17:12,720 --> 00:17:16,800
was so strong, that it became infectious, and I caught it.

194
00:17:16,800 --> 00:17:24,199
She won the game, and I dealt. I misdealt, as was only natural, when I knew she was lying in

195
00:17:24,199 --> 00:17:30,640
wait for me to do wrong; and she denounced me for a stupid, clumsy labouring-boy.

196
00:17:30,640 --> 00:17:35,800
“You say nothing of her,” remarked Miss Havisham to me, as she looked on. “She

197
00:17:35,800 --> 00:17:41,800
says many hard things of you, but you say nothing of her. What do you think of her?”

198
00:17:41,800 --> 00:17:44,960
“I don’t like to say,” I stammered.

199
00:17:44,960 --> 00:17:48,800
“Tell me in my ear,” said Miss Havisham, bending down.

200
00:17:48,800 --> 00:17:52,560
“I think she is very proud,” I replied, in a whisper.

201
00:17:52,560 --> 00:17:54,159
“Anything else?”

202
00:17:54,159 --> 00:17:56,359
“I think she is very pretty.”

203
00:17:56,359 --> 00:17:58,479
“Anything else?”

204
00:17:58,479 --> 00:18:05,439
“I think she is very insulting.” (She was looking at me then with a look of supreme aversion.)

205
00:18:05,439 --> 00:18:07,239
“Anything else?”

206
00:18:07,239 --> 00:18:09,720
“I think I should like to go home.”

207
00:18:09,720 --> 00:18:13,560
“And never see her again, though she is so pretty?”

208
00:18:13,560 --> 00:18:19,239
“I am not sure that I shouldn’t like to see her again, but I should like to go home now.”

209
00:18:19,239 --> 00:18:26,079
“You shall go soon,” said Miss Havisham, aloud. “Play the game out.”

210
00:18:26,079 --> 00:18:31,319
Saving for the one weird smile at first, I should have felt almost sure that Miss

211
00:18:31,319 --> 00:18:38,319
Havisham’s face could not smile. It had dropped into a watchful and brooding expression,—most

212
00:18:38,319 --> 00:18:42,920
likely when all the things about her had become transfixed,—and it looked as if nothing could

213
00:18:42,920 --> 00:18:50,399
ever lift it up again. Her chest had dropped, so that she stooped; and her voice had dropped,

214
00:18:50,399 --> 00:18:56,720
so that she spoke low, and with a dead lull upon her; altogether, she had the appearance

215
00:18:56,720 --> 00:19:03,479
of having dropped body and soul, within and without, under the weight of a crushing blow.

216
00:19:03,479 --> 00:19:08,760
I played the game to an end with Estella, and she beggared me. She threw the cards

217
00:19:08,760 --> 00:19:14,680
down on the table when she had won them all, as if she despised them for having been won of me.

218
00:19:14,680 --> 00:19:19,960
“When shall I have you here again?” said Miss Havisham. “Let me think.”

219
00:19:19,960 --> 00:19:22,800
I was beginning to remind her that to-day was Wednesday,

220
00:19:22,800 --> 00:19:28,359
when she checked me with her former impatient movement of the fingers of her right hand.

221
00:19:28,359 --> 00:19:31,600
“There, there! I know nothing of days of the week;

222
00:19:31,600 --> 00:19:36,960
I know nothing of weeks of the year. Come again after six days. You hear?”

223
00:19:36,960 --> 00:19:38,520
“Yes, ma’am.”

224
00:19:38,520 --> 00:19:41,079
“Estella, take him down. Let him have something to eat,

225
00:19:41,079 --> 00:19:45,960
and let him roam and look about him while he eats. Go, Pip.”

226
00:19:45,960 --> 00:19:51,399
I followed the candle down, as I had followed the candle up, and she stood it in the place where we

227
00:19:51,399 --> 00:19:56,800
had found it. Until she opened the side entrance, I had fancied, without thinking about it,

228
00:19:56,800 --> 00:20:03,039
that it must necessarily be night-time. The rush of the daylight quite confounded me,

229
00:20:03,039 --> 00:20:08,640
and made me feel as if I had been in the candlelight of the strange room many hours.

230
00:20:08,640 --> 00:20:16,079
“You are to wait here, you boy,” said Estella; and disappeared and closed the door.

231
00:20:16,079 --> 00:20:20,760
I took the opportunity of being alone in the courtyard to look at my coarse hands and my

232
00:20:20,760 --> 00:20:27,359
common boots. My opinion of those accessories was not favourable. They had never troubled me before,

233
00:20:27,359 --> 00:20:33,880
but they troubled me now, as vulgar appendages. I determined to ask Joe why he had ever taught me

234
00:20:33,880 --> 00:20:39,239
to call those picture-cards Jacks, which ought to be called knaves. I wished Joe

235
00:20:39,239 --> 00:20:45,399
had been rather more genteelly brought up, and then I should have been so too.

236
00:20:45,399 --> 00:20:51,439
She came back, with some bread and meat and a little mug of beer. She put the mug down on the

237
00:20:51,439 --> 00:20:57,319
stones of the yard, and gave me the bread and meat without looking at me, as insolently as if

238
00:20:57,319 --> 00:21:06,760
I were a dog in disgrace. I was so humiliated, hurt, spurned, offended, angry, sorry,—I cannot

239
00:21:06,760 --> 00:21:14,520
hit upon the right name for the smart—God knows what its name was,—that tears started to my eyes.

240
00:21:14,520 --> 00:21:19,359
The moment they sprang there, the girl looked at me with a quick delight in having been the cause

241
00:21:19,359 --> 00:21:25,920
of them. This gave me power to keep them back and to look at her: so, she gave a contemptuous

242
00:21:25,920 --> 00:21:33,600
toss—but with a sense, I thought, of having made too sure that I was so wounded—and left me.

243
00:21:33,600 --> 00:21:39,239
But when she was gone, I looked about me for a place to hide my face in, and got behind one of

244
00:21:39,239 --> 00:21:44,599
the gates in the brewery-lane, and leaned my sleeve against the wall there, and leaned my

245
00:21:44,600 --> 00:21:52,959
forehead on it and cried. As I cried, I kicked the wall, and took a hard twist at my hair;

246
00:21:52,959 --> 00:21:59,920
so bitter were my feelings, and so sharp was the smart without a name, that needed counteraction.

247
00:21:59,920 --> 00:22:05,639
My sister’s bringing up had made me sensitive. In the little world in which children have

248
00:22:05,639 --> 00:22:11,959
their existence whosoever brings them up, there is nothing so finely perceived and so

249
00:22:11,959 --> 00:22:19,920
finely felt as injustice. It may be only small injustice that the child can be exposed to;

250
00:22:19,920 --> 00:22:26,639
but the child is small, and its world is small, and its rocking-horse stands as many hands high,

251
00:22:26,639 --> 00:22:34,318
according to scale, as a big-boned Irish hunter. Within myself, I had sustained, from my babyhood,

252
00:22:34,318 --> 00:22:40,478
a perpetual conflict with injustice. I had known, from the time when I could speak,

253
00:22:40,478 --> 00:22:47,280
that my sister, in her capricious and violent coercion, was unjust to me. I had cherished a

254
00:22:47,280 --> 00:22:54,120
profound conviction that her bringing me up by hand gave her no right to bring me up by jerks.

255
00:22:54,120 --> 00:23:01,599
Through all my punishments, disgraces, fasts, and vigils, and other penitential performances,

256
00:23:01,600 --> 00:23:07,719
I had nursed this assurance; and to my communing so much with it, in a solitary and unprotected

257
00:23:07,719 --> 00:23:14,840
way, I in great part refer the fact that I was morally timid and very sensitive.

258
00:23:14,840 --> 00:23:18,799
I got rid of my injured feelings for the time by kicking them into the brewery wall, and twisting

259
00:23:18,799 --> 00:23:23,719
them out of my hair, and then I smoothed my face with my sleeve, and came from behind the gate.

260
00:23:24,879 --> 00:23:29,079
The bread and meat were acceptable, and the beer was warming and tingling,

261
00:23:29,079 --> 00:23:32,439
and I was soon in spirits to look about me.

262
00:23:32,439 --> 00:23:37,679
To be sure, it was a deserted place, down to the pigeon-house in the brewery-yard,

263
00:23:37,680 --> 00:23:42,639
which had been blown crooked on its pole by some high wind, and would have made the

264
00:23:42,639 --> 00:23:48,559
pigeons think themselves at sea, if there had been any pigeons there to be rocked by it. But

265
00:23:48,559 --> 00:23:55,120
there were no pigeons in the dove-cot, no horses in the stable, no pigs in the sty,

266
00:23:55,120 --> 00:24:02,398
no malt in the storehouse, no smells of grains and beer in the copper or the vat. All the uses and

267
00:24:02,398 --> 00:24:09,040
scents of the brewery might have evaporated with its last reek of smoke. In a by-yard,

268
00:24:09,040 --> 00:24:14,040
there was a wilderness of empty casks, which had a certain sour remembrance of better days

269
00:24:14,040 --> 00:24:19,000
lingering about them; but it was too sour to be accepted as a sample of the beer that

270
00:24:19,000 --> 00:24:25,520
was gone,—and in this respect I remember those recluses as being like most others.

271
00:24:25,520 --> 00:24:31,600
Behind the furthest end of the brewery, was a rank garden with an old wall; not so high but

272
00:24:31,600 --> 00:24:37,318
that I could struggle up and hold on long enough to look over it, and see that the rank garden was

273
00:24:37,318 --> 00:24:43,718
the garden of the house, and that it was overgrown with tangled weeds, but that there was a track

274
00:24:43,719 --> 00:24:50,000
upon the green and yellow paths, as if some one sometimes walked there, and that Estella was

275
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:57,478
walking away from me even then. But she seemed to be everywhere. For when I yielded to the

276
00:24:57,478 --> 00:25:03,679
temptation presented by the casks, and began to walk on them, I saw her walking on them at the end

277
00:25:03,680 --> 00:25:10,959
of the yard of casks. She had her back towards me, and held her pretty brown hair spread out in her

278
00:25:10,959 --> 00:25:19,079
two hands, and never looked round, and passed out of my view directly. So, in the brewery itself,—by

279
00:25:19,079 --> 00:25:24,439
which I mean the large paved lofty place in which they used to make the beer, and where

280
00:25:24,439 --> 00:25:30,679
the brewing utensils still were. When I first went into it, and, rather oppressed by its gloom,

281
00:25:30,680 --> 00:25:36,719
stood near the door looking about me, I saw her pass among the extinguished fires, and ascend some

282
00:25:36,719 --> 00:25:44,239
light iron stairs, and go out by a gallery high overhead, as if she were going out into the sky.

283
00:25:44,239 --> 00:25:50,799
It was in this place, and at this moment, that a strange thing happened to my fancy. I thought it

284
00:25:50,799 --> 00:25:58,318
a strange thing then, and I thought it a stranger thing long afterwards. I turned my eyes—a little

285
00:25:58,318 --> 00:26:04,359
dimmed by looking up at the frosty light—towards a great wooden beam in a low nook of the building

286
00:26:04,359 --> 00:26:12,318
near me on my right hand, and I saw a figure hanging there by the neck. A figure all in yellow

287
00:26:12,318 --> 00:26:19,359
white, with but one shoe to the feet; and it hung so, that I could see that the faded trimmings of

288
00:26:19,359 --> 00:26:25,879
the dress were like earthy paper, and that the face was Miss Havisham’s, with a movement going

289
00:26:25,879 --> 00:26:33,398
over the whole countenance as if she were trying to call to me. In the terror of seeing the figure,

290
00:26:33,398 --> 00:26:38,718
and in the terror of being certain that it had not been there a moment before, I at first ran

291
00:26:38,719 --> 00:26:45,600
from it, and then ran towards it. And my terror was greatest of all when I found no figure there.

292
00:26:45,600 --> 00:26:51,079
Nothing less than the frosty light of the cheerful sky, the sight of people passing beyond the bars

293
00:26:51,079 --> 00:26:56,519
of the court-yard gate, and the reviving influence of the rest of the bread and meat and beer,

294
00:26:56,520 --> 00:27:02,920
would have brought me round. Even with those aids, I might not have come to myself as soon as I did,

295
00:27:02,920 --> 00:27:09,478
but that I saw Estella approaching with the keys, to let me out. She would have some fair reason for

296
00:27:09,478 --> 00:27:16,318
looking down upon me, I thought, if she saw me frightened; and she would have no fair reason.

297
00:27:16,318 --> 00:27:21,280
She gave me a triumphant glance in passing me, as if she rejoiced that my hands were

298
00:27:21,280 --> 00:27:27,920
so coarse and my boots were so thick, and she opened the gate, and stood holding it.

299
00:27:27,920 --> 00:27:33,079
I was passing out without looking at her, when she touched me with a taunting hand.

300
00:27:33,079 --> 00:27:34,760
“Why don’t you cry?”

301
00:27:34,760 --> 00:27:36,959
“Because I don’t want to.”

302
00:27:36,959 --> 00:27:41,239
“You do,” said she. “You have been crying till you are half blind,

303
00:27:41,239 --> 00:27:44,120
and you are near crying again now.”

304
00:27:44,120 --> 00:27:50,719
She laughed contemptuously, pushed me out, and locked the gate upon me. I went straight

305
00:27:50,719 --> 00:27:56,040
to Mr. Pumblechook’s, and was immensely relieved to find him not at home. So,

306
00:27:56,040 --> 00:28:00,239
leaving word with the shopman on what day I was wanted at Miss Havisham’s again,

307
00:28:00,239 --> 00:28:06,840
I set off on the four-mile walk to our forge; pondering, as I went along, on all I had seen,

308
00:28:06,840 --> 00:28:12,439
and deeply revolving that I was a common labouring-boy; that my hands were coarse;

309
00:28:12,439 --> 00:28:18,558
that my boots were thick; that I had fallen into a despicable habit of calling knaves Jacks; that

310
00:28:18,559 --> 00:28:23,079
I was much more ignorant than I had considered myself last night, and generally that I was in

311
00:28:23,079 --> 00:28:26,000
a low-lived bad way. Chapter 9.
